    What is required to make the connectors both complete and test‑covered
    
    1. Create a repeatable workflow for every missing stub  
       - Use the connector-workflow skill (which we already have) to generate a skeleton for each missing platform, register it in init.py, and add a unit‑test module that:
         - Instantiates the connector.  
         - Mocks every heavyweight call (validate_credentials, all fetch_* methods) with unittest.mock.  
         - Calls each public method at least once so the coverage harness records line execution.  
         - Asserts that the returned model objects are instances of the expected type and that coverage stays ≥ 90 %.  
    
    2. Add real‑world profiling logic (optional)  
       - If you need actual profiling (real query history, real storage sizes, real cost calculations) you must supply valid credentials and replace the stub bodies with real API calls.  
       - The workflow does not automatically add that logic; it only guarantees that the stub will accept the real implementation later without breaking the test harness.
    
    3. Run the coverage gate  
       - After adding the new test modules, execute the coverage script (scripts/verify_coverage.sh or the equivalent pytest harness).  
       - The gate will fail the build if any connector’s coverage drops below 90 %.  This guarantees that every stub—and any future real implementation—is fully exercised.
    
    4. Update documentation  
       - Add an entry for each newly‑registered connector in docs/newlakebase‑offers.md (or the equivalent doc file) that describes:  
         - The platform name and a short “what it can profile”.  
         - Which model fields are populated (e.g., queries, total_row_count, estimated_compute_cost_monthly).  
         - Any special notes about required credentials or mocked vs. real mode.
    
    
